// Fixture for the `oskel build` qualified-native error test
// (tests/build_error.cmake, PHASE 7f.2).
//
// `modules.hostclock.clock` is a `native fn` reached through the module
// namespace. The read goes through the module table with `GETTABLE`, not with
// `GETGLOBAL`, so before PHASE 7f.2 the lowerer could not see the use and
// emitted a binary that faulted at run time (`cannot call a non-function
// value`) instead of failing the build. The compiler now records the use on the
// proto that performs it, so the AOT backend must reject it with the dedicated
// message, exactly like the unqualified `native fn clock()` case.
//
// `clock` is a host builtin with no curated AOT stub (unlike the `std.math`
// libm surface, which PHASE 7c.5b.4 resolves from the embedded musl ports), so
// it is the binding that still exercises the rejection. The program runs on the
// VM (the host registry resolves `clock`), so it doubles as a script test there.
import modules.hostclock;

print(modules.hostclock.clock() > 0);
